---
source: tests/help.rs
expression: "String::from_utf8(output.stdout).unwrap()"
---
Read INPUT (or standard input), compare adjacent lines, and write one copy of each run of identical
lines to OUTPUT (or standard output). Repeated lines are only detected when they are adjacent, so
sort the input first to find all duplicates.

A drop-in clone of macOS uniq: the same options, the same output byte for byte, the same error
messages and exit status. The obsolete forms -NUM (same as -f NUM) and +NUM (same as -s NUM) are
accepted. Option parsing stops at the first operand.

Comparisons follow the locale (LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE, LANG): in a UTF-8 locale -i folds Unicode case, -s
counts characters and -f treats Unicode blanks as separators; in the C locale they work on bytes.

Usage: uniqr [OPTIONS] [INPUT] [OUTPUT]

Arguments:
  [INPUT]
          File to read; - or absent reads standard input

  [OUTPUT]
          File to write (created or truncated); absent writes standard output

Options:
  -c, --count
          Precede each output line with the number of times it occurred and a space

  -d, --repeated
          Output one copy of each repeated line; ignored with -D

  -D, --all-repeated[=<SEPTYPE>]
          Output every copy of each repeated line; SEPTYPE (attached: -Dprepend,
          --all-repeated=separate) adds a blank line before (prepend) or after (separate) each group
          [default: none]
          
          [possible values: none, prepend, separate]

  -f, --skip-fields <NUM>
          Ignore the first NUM fields (blank-separated) when comparing

  -i, --ignore-case
          Compare lines case-insensitively

  -s, --skip-chars <CHARS>
          Ignore the first CHARS characters when comparing (after any skipped fields)

  -u, --unique
          Only output lines that are not repeated

      --help
          Print help

      --version
          Print version

Examples:
  # Count how often each line occurs
  $ sort cities.txt | uniqr -c
  # Show lines repeated in a row, ignoring case
  $ uniqr -d -i names.txt
  # Print every repeated group, blank line after each, ignoring the first field
  $ uniqr -Dseparate -f 1 log.txt

Exit codes:
    0  Success
    1  General error
    2  Invalid arguments or usage
  130  Interrupted (Ctrl-C)
